    "overview": "Learners begin by recalling that after the discounts the actual sales were KES 18,000, yet Wafula Barasa counted only KES 17,370 in the cash box. They predict where the remaining KES 630 went, and brainstorm who in Kenya is paid commission. All figures are illustrative (made-up).\n\nIn the observe phase learners study Halima Mohamed's T-shirt sales: 40 T-shirts sold at KES 315 each, giving sales of KES 12,600 after the 10% discount. She earns 5% commission. Learners calculate 5% of 12,600 = 630, then reverse the process: 630 / 12,600 x 100 = 5%. They then practise with a second illustrative case, a Kisumu salesperson who earns 8% on sales of KES 25,000, which is KES 2,000.\n\nIn the explain phase learners subtract: 18,000 - 630 = 17,370, matching the cash box. With Lesson 3's discount of 2,000, the gap of 2,630 is now explained. Learners add questions about profit and about the Mombasa trip to the Driving Question Board and revise their model by adding commission as a payment out of the cash box. Profit is not calculated until Lesson 5.",
