    "overview": "In this final lesson learners return to the puzzle that began the unit: the Mazingira Club of Lake View Secondary School, Kisumu, budgeted KES 20,000 for its open-day stall, sold every item, yet Wafula Barasa counted only KES 17,370 in the cash box, and Mr Daniel Brooks got USD 98.84 back from USD 100 at Nyali Forex Bureau in Mombasa. All figures are illustrative. Learners first predict, in writing, how much of each gap each idea explains, then collect the evidence from Lessons 1-7 into one reference table.\n\nIn the Explain phase learners build the final money model in groups: the planned budget (income 20,000, expenditure 15,000, surplus 5,000), the discount of KES 2,000 (10% off), the commission of KES 630 (5% of 12,600), the cash box of 17,370 and gap of 2,630, the profit of 3,600 (25% on cost 14,400) against the net surplus of 2,370, the trip deficit of 1,515 against the guide fee of 3,885, and the buying/selling gap. They then present outcome (f): where commercial arithmetic is used in daily life. The Driving Question Board is completed by marking each question answered, and learners compare their Lesson 1 model with the final model.\n\nThe lesson ends with the Final Explanation task, written individually and marked with a four-level rubric, answering the driving question: how can the club use budgeting, discounts, commission, profit and loss and currency exchange to plan so that its money is not less than expected? The ideas used are only those taught in Lessons 1-7. Minutes: Predict 5 + Observe 8 + Explain 10 + DQB 5 + Model Building 12 = 40 minutes.",
